### name: txt.rejects-binary
--- note
Text functions do not silently decode bytes; FROM_UTF8 is the explicit door.
--- source
LEN(FROM_HEX("41"))
--- expect
error E_NOT_TEXT
===
### name: txt.substr.length-beyond-any-string
--- note
A length larger than the string returns the rest of it. In C++ the count
saturates at the maximum integer, so `start + len` was signed overflow and
returned "" instead.
--- source
SUBSTR("abcdef", 2, 9223372036854775807)
--- expect
text "bcdef"
===
### name: txt.find.start-beyond-any-string
--- source
FIND("ab", "abc", 9223372036854775807)
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: txt.find.is-one-based
--- note
FIND's optional start is 1-based like SUBSTR's, and 0 is out of its domain
rather than a synonym for the beginning. The sibling case is
txt.substr.is-one-based; this one had no coverage at all until the arity and
misuse sweep went looking for the gaps its own template left.
--- source
FIND("a", "banana", 0)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:21
===
### name: txt.find.negative-start
--- source
FIND("a", "banana", -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:21
===
### name: text.upper.dotless-i-is-left-alone
--- note
UPPER and LOWER are ASCII-only by specification, and the Turkish dotless i is
where every host's native case function disagrees with that. Python's
str.upper() maps U+0131 to ASCII I; Common Lisp's char-upcase does the same;
PHP's strtoupper does not, and mb_strtoupper does. Two hosts carry an
ascii_upper of their own PRECISELY because of this character, and the comment
in python/sel/sql/bindings.py says so -- but no case named it, so the
discipline was documented and unasserted.

If a host ever reaches for its language's own case function, this is the case
that fails. Eszett is the same class and already pinned; this is the other
half.
--- source
UPPER("ı")
--- expect
text "ı"
===
### name: text.lower.dotted-capital-i-is-left-alone
--- note
The other direction. U+0130 lower-cases to "i" plus a combining dot above
under full Unicode rules, which is one character becoming two -- a length
change out of a case fold, which is the thing ASCII-only exists to avoid.
--- source
LOWER("İ")
--- expect
text "İ"
===
### name: text.upper.ascii-i-still-folds
--- note
The control for the two above: ASCII-only means ASCII still folds. Without
this, a host that simply returned its argument would pass both of them.
--- source
UPPER("i")
--- expect
text "I"
===
